---
// Lesson 6.1a — Gravity & Jump: AddForce Impulse
// The platformer begins: Space, AddForce Impulse, gravityScale tuning.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>6.1a — Gravity &amp; Jump: AddForce Impulse</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    You are in the cave now (<a href="5.3a.html">5.3a</a>), and caves
    have holes, ledges, and reasons to leave the ground. Today you give
    your character a jump — one line of physics — and then spend the
    rest of the hour tuning what that line <em>feels</em> like.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Predict First</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>Read the jump branch inside PlayerController.Update — the part
    that only exists because isPlatformer is true:</p>
    <pre><code>[SerializeField] private float jumpForce = 10f;
private Rigidbody2D rb;

...

if (isPlatformer)
{
    if (Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.Space))
    {
        rb.AddForce(Vector2.up * jumpForce, ForceMode2D.Impulse);
    }
}</code></pre>

    <p>
        Predict before running: AddForce with mode Impulse applies an
        INSTANT kick, not a steady push — like a flick, not a hand.
        So why does the player keep RISING after the flick ends? What
        field from <a href="5.3a.html">5.3a</a> pulls them back down,
        and what number controls how floaty the arc is?
    </p>
    <p>
        Second prediction: you hold Space down for two whole seconds.
        One jump or several? Why does GetKeyDown (not GetKey) decide
        this — and what would rocket boots feel like with GetKey?
    </p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            Every jump you have ever felt was three numbers arguing:
            jumpForce up, gravityScale down, mass resisting. Which
            two of those live on the player in the Inspector right
            now — and which one would you tune FIRST to make the jump
            feel heavy rather than floaty?
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — the flick.</strong> In the Platformer scene,
    press Play and tap Space. If the jump already works, do NOT move
    on: you are going to rebuild it anyway. Comment out the AddForce
    line, tap Space (nothing — you are a top-down ghost in a
    platformer body), and retype it. That gap — "nothing" then
    "flight" — is the whole lesson. AddForce says: apply this force
    NOW, physics takes over. Impulse means the "now" is one frame,
    not a dribble.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — the tuning triangle.</strong> Three knobs, one
    jump. Change ONE at a time and write one sentence of feel:
    <table>
        <tr><th>Knob</th><th>Try</th><th>Feel sentence</th></tr>
        <tr><td>jumpForce</td><td>10 → 15 → 5</td><td>"how high" — arcade vs. ant</td></tr>
        <tr><td>rb.gravityScale</td><td>2 → 4 → 1</td><td>"how heavy" — moon vs. balloon</td></tr>
        <tr><td>rb.mass</td><td>1 → 5 → 0.5</td><td>how much the SAME force moves you</td></tr>
    </table>
    Note the trap: raising mass makes you fall faster AND jump lower.
    The starter keeps mass at 1 and tunes the other two — that is the
    convention most platformers use: keep one knob boring so the other
    two mean something.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — read the arc.</strong> Jump next to a wall and
    trace the shape your character draws. It is a parabola: the flick
    gives upward velocity, gravity subtracts from it every frame
    (that is literally what gravityScale does — a multiplier on the
    engine's 9.81 m/s²). Halfway up, velocity passes through zero —
    that hang time at the apex is the floatiest moment in the game,
    and 6.3a will weaponize it.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: The broken jump</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Delete the <code>ForceMode2D.Impulse</code> argument
                (keep the force). Tap Space. Describe the difference
                — and explain it using "one frame vs. dribble."</li>
            <li>Swap Vector2.up for Vector2.down. What did you build
                instead of a jump — and what kind of game mechanic
                IS that (a stomp!)?</li>
            <li>Set gravityScale to 0 in the Platformer scene (the
                branch in Start sets it for you — fight it). What
                happens after ONE jump, and why does "no gravity" and
                "no jump" end in the same place?</li>
            <li>Change GetKeyDown to GetKey and hold Space. Count the
                jumps per second — and why will
                <a href="6.2a.html">6.2a</a> make this bug impossible
                rather than merely annoying?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Jump design</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Tune a "sticky" jump: gravityScale 4, jumpForce 14.
                Then a "floaty" one: 1.5 and 8. Which fits a cave
                full of spikes — and why do most action games choose
                sticky?</li>
            <li>Variable jump height (the classic): release Space
                early to jump LOW. What would you cut off mid-flight
                — hint: the rb's velocity.y? Why is that fairer than
                it sounds?</li>
            <li>Hard: jump on a MOVING platform. What does AddForce
                do to the platform's velocity — and why do real games
                parent the rider instead?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Explain Impulse vs. continuous force in feel-words</li>
            <li>Tune jumpForce / gravityScale / mass and say what each changes</li>
            <li>Explain the parabola: flick up, gravity subtracts, apex, fall</li>
            <li>Explain GetKeyDown vs GetKey for one-shot actions</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-06/index.html">Unit 6: Jumping</a> — the jump feel guide</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — AddForce, ForceMode2D</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="6.2a.html">6.2a — Ground Checks: No Infinite Jumps</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="6.1a" title="6.1a Gravity &amp; Jump: AddForce Impulse — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
